\description{
Tests whether the block Y is independent of the block X given the blocks V
(\code{Y ~ X | V1 + V2 + ...}), with the Computational Conditional Independence (CCI)
approach: compare how well Y is predicted on held-out rows with and without X,
and calibrate that comparison against resampled copies of X.
}
\details{
\itemize{
\item \strong{Statistic:} \code{T = RMSEP(Y ~ V + X) - RMSEP(Y ~ V)} on the test rows of a
random train/test split (\code{\link[=cci_test_gen]{cci_test_gen()}}). Negative \code{T} means that X improves
the prediction of Y beyond V.
\item \strong{Null distribution:} \code{nperm} repetitions of the same procedure on a resampled
X* (\code{\link[=cci_null_dist]{cci_null_dist()}}): rotation (default), permutation or conditional
permutation of X, chosen with \code{resampling}.
\item \strong{p-value:} left tail, the share of null statistics at or below the observed
one (\code{pvalue = "empirical"}), or from a normal fit to the null statistics
(\code{pvalue = "parametric"}). By default the observed statistic comes from one
random train/test split. With \code{npval > 1} (experimental) it is computed on
\code{npval} splits, each is compared with the same null statistics and the p-values
are averaged.
}

The regression behind M0 (Y ~ V) and M1 (Y ~ V + X) is chosen with \code{method}:
\itemize{
\item \code{"PLS"}(default): ordinary PLS (\code{pls::plsr}) on the \code{cbind}-ed blocks; the number of
components of each model is chosen by inner CV.
\item \code{"SOPLS"} : sequential and orthogonalised PLS (\code{multiblock::sopls});
each V block is a separate block. With \code{ncomp = "auto"} each V block gets one
component and the number of X components is chosen by inner CV.
\item \code{"ROSA"}: response-oriented sequential alternation (\code{multiblock::rosa}); every
block is kept separate and each component comes from the block that best
explains the current Y residual. Components chosen by inner CV.
\item \code{"XGB"}: multi-target XGBoost (experimental; needs xgboost >= 2.0).
\item \code{"RF"}: multi-output random forest (\code{grf::multi_regression_forest}; needs grf).
\item \code{"KNN"}: multi-output k nearest neighbour regression (needs FNN); k chosen by
inner CV.
\item a learner function with the interface described in \code{R/learners.R}. New methods
can be made available by name by adding them to \code{.cci_methods} in that file.
}

With many conditioning variables, \code{reduce = "pca"} or \code{"pls"} replaces the V blocks
by a few scores before the regression (fitted on the training rows of every split),
so that a nonlinear learner works on a low-dimensional input; see \code{\link[=cci_test_gen]{cci_test_gen()}}.
The resampling of X still adjusts for the full V.

All tuning (components, ...) uses the training rows only, identically for the
observed and the resampled X. See \code{\link[=cci_test_gen]{cci_test_gen()}} for the details of each
method and its options.
}
